1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,827
Push off a wall in a swimming
pool, and you glide away.

2
00:00:03,827 --> 00:00:06,788
Jump off a small boat,
and the boat slides back.

3
00:00:07,188 --> 00:00:10,537
You push one way, and something
pushes you the other way.

4
00:00:10,537 --> 00:00:11,437
Every time.

5
00:00:11,785 --> 00:00:14,997
Aarav sits on his rolling
chair at the study table.

6
00:00:14,997 --> 00:00:16,840
He pushes the table, hard.

7
00:00:17,190 --> 00:00:18,828
The table hardly moves.

8
00:00:18,828 --> 00:00:21,595
But Aarav rolls backwards across the room.

9
00:00:21,595 --> 00:00:23,084
He pushed the table.

10
00:00:23,084 --> 00:00:24,522
So what pushed him?

11
00:00:25,022 --> 00:00:27,197
The answer is Newton's third law.

12
00:00:27,197 --> 00:00:30,386
And it hides a trap that
catches almost everyone.

13
00:00:31,036 --> 00:00:32,173
Here is the law.

14
00:00:32,523 --> 00:00:36,639
To every action, there is always
an equal and opposite reaction.

15
00:00:37,039 --> 00:00:40,076
In plain words, forces
always come in pairs.

16
00:00:40,076 --> 00:00:42,773
If A pushes B, then B pushes A back.

17
00:00:43,173 --> 00:00:47,612
The two forces have the same size
and opposite directions, and they act

18
00:00:47,612 --> 00:00:49,088
at the same instant.

19
00:00:49,088 --> 00:00:50,762
Neither one comes first.

20
00:00:51,162 --> 00:00:55,142
So the table pushed Aarav back,
just as hard as he pushed it.

21
00:00:55,142 --> 00:00:56,703
His chair rolls easily.

22
00:00:56,703 --> 00:00:58,760
The table's legs grip the floor.

23
00:00:59,410 --> 00:01:00,948
To the motion notebook.

24
00:01:00,948 --> 00:01:02,833
Two carts on a smooth track.

25
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:05,009
A is two kilograms, B is eight.

26
00:01:05,009 --> 00:01:07,269
A squeezed spring sits between them.

27
00:01:07,619 --> 00:01:08,519
Let go!

28
00:01:08,381 --> 00:01:09,630
They fly apart.

29
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:11,917
A shoots left, B rolls right.

30
00:01:12,317 --> 00:01:13,294
Slow-mo replay.

31
00:01:13,294 --> 00:01:16,681
Freeze in the middle of the
push, and draw the forces.

32
00:01:17,031 --> 00:01:20,409
The spring pushes A to the
left, and B to the right.

33
00:01:20,409 --> 00:01:24,855
On the readout, both forces are exactly
the same size, at every instant.

34
00:01:25,255 --> 00:01:27,626
But the accelerations are not equal.

35
00:01:27,626 --> 00:01:32,241
B has four times the mass, so it
gets one quarter of the acceleration.

36
00:01:32,641 --> 00:01:35,875
On the graph, the two force
curves are mirror images.

37
00:01:35,875 --> 00:01:36,989
Now let it run.

38
00:01:36,989 --> 00:01:39,298
When one shrinks, so does the other.

39
00:01:39,298 --> 00:01:40,493
They end together.

40
00:01:40,893 --> 00:01:43,277
A leaves at four metres per second.

41
00:01:43,277 --> 00:01:44,853
B leaves at only one.

42
00:01:44,853 --> 00:01:47,216
Same force, very different effects.

43
00:01:47,866 --> 00:01:48,766
Your turn.

44
00:01:48,735 --> 00:01:51,152
A heavy truck hits a small scooter.

45
00:01:51,152 --> 00:01:53,409
Which one feels the bigger force?

46
00:01:53,409 --> 00:01:54,688
Pause and decide.

47
00:01:57,888 --> 00:01:58,788
Neither!

48
00:01:58,589 --> 00:02:00,111
The forces are equal.

49
00:02:00,111 --> 00:02:04,686
The scooter has much less mass, so
it gets a much bigger acceleration.

50
00:02:05,336 --> 00:02:08,009
Now let's write it down,
one step at a time.

51
00:02:08,359 --> 00:02:09,259
Step one.

52
00:02:09,219 --> 00:02:14,423
Write F sub AB for the force on A
by B, and F sub BA for the force on

53
00:02:14,423 --> 00:02:15,323
B by A.

54
00:02:15,991 --> 00:02:16,921
Step two.

55
00:02:16,921 --> 00:02:21,107
The third law says F sub
AB equals minus F sub BA.

56
00:02:21,107 --> 00:02:23,477
Same size, opposite direction.

57
00:02:24,227 --> 00:02:25,243
Step three.

58
00:02:25,243 --> 00:02:28,134
The two forces act on different bodies.

59
00:02:28,134 --> 00:02:31,676
So use the second law
on each body on its own.

60
00:02:31,676 --> 00:02:34,534
The acceleration of A is F over m A.

61
00:02:34,534 --> 00:02:36,255
Of B, F over m B.

62
00:02:37,005 --> 00:02:37,930
Step four.

63
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:38,961
Divide them.

64
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:41,681
a A over a B equals m B over m A.

65
00:02:41,681 --> 00:02:44,838
The lighter body gets
the bigger acceleration.

66
00:02:45,588 --> 00:02:48,139
For our carts, that is eight over two.

67
00:02:48,139 --> 00:02:50,298
Four times the acceleration for A.

68
00:02:50,898 --> 00:02:51,933
Remember this.

69
00:02:51,933 --> 00:02:55,059
Equal and opposite, but
on two different bodies.

70
00:02:55,759 --> 00:02:57,637
Here is why that matters.

71
00:02:57,637 --> 00:02:58,764
Side by side.

72
00:02:59,164 --> 00:03:01,351
On the left, a book on a table.

73
00:03:01,351 --> 00:03:03,006
Its weight pulls it down.

74
00:03:03,006 --> 00:03:04,569
The table pushes it up.

75
00:03:04,569 --> 00:03:07,410
Both forces act on the
book, so they cancel.

76
00:03:07,810 --> 00:03:10,223
On the right, an action reaction pair.

77
00:03:10,223 --> 00:03:12,364
The book pushes down on the table.

78
00:03:12,364 --> 00:03:14,416
The table pushes up on the book.

79
00:03:14,416 --> 00:03:17,254
One force on each body,
so they never cancel.

80
00:03:17,854 --> 00:03:18,835
Now you try.

81
00:03:18,835 --> 00:03:22,559
The book's weight and the table's
push are equal and opposite.

82
00:03:22,559 --> 00:03:24,711
Are they an action reaction pair?

83
00:03:24,711 --> 00:03:25,930
Pause and decide.

84
00:03:29,130 --> 00:03:30,030
No!

85
00:03:29,546 --> 00:03:30,990
Both act on the book.

86
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:35,234
The partner of its weight is the book
pulling the Earth up, with the same

87
00:03:35,234 --> 00:03:36,134
force.

88
00:03:36,435 --> 00:03:38,171
Now, one worked example.

89
00:03:38,521 --> 00:03:42,491
Aarav, sixty kilograms, stands
on skates on a smooth floor.

90
00:03:42,491 --> 00:03:47,375
He pushes his friend, forty kilograms,
with one hundred and twenty newtons.

91
00:03:47,375 --> 00:03:49,251
Find each one's acceleration.

92
00:03:49,751 --> 00:03:51,517
The friend gets the push.

93
00:03:51,517 --> 00:03:54,076
One twenty newtons over forty kilograms.

94
00:03:54,076 --> 00:03:56,782
Three metres per second squared, forward.

95
00:03:57,532 --> 00:04:01,546
By the third law, the friend pushes
Aarav back with one twenty newtons

96
00:04:01,546 --> 00:04:02,446
too.

97
00:04:02,758 --> 00:04:06,055
So Aarav's acceleration
is one twenty over sixty.

98
00:04:06,055 --> 00:04:08,962
Two metres per second squared, backwards.

99
00:04:09,712 --> 00:04:11,103
Same force on both.

100
00:04:11,103 --> 00:04:13,334
The lighter friend speeds up more.

101
00:04:13,984 --> 00:04:15,449
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:15,799 --> 00:04:20,402
Many students say: a horse pulls a
cart, and the cart pulls the horse back

103
00:04:20,402 --> 00:04:21,302
equally.

104
00:04:21,076 --> 00:04:23,316
They cancel, so nothing can move.

105
00:04:23,716 --> 00:04:26,470
But the two forces act
on different bodies.

106
00:04:26,470 --> 00:04:30,324
To see if the cart moves, add
only the forces on the cart.

107
00:04:30,974 --> 00:04:32,422
Where is this asked?

108
00:04:32,422 --> 00:04:36,662
In concept questions that spot action
reaction pairs, and in numericals

109
00:04:36,662 --> 00:04:38,762
on bodies that push each other.

110
00:04:39,362 --> 00:04:40,555
Exam level now.

111
00:04:40,555 --> 00:04:45,389
Block A, two kilograms, and block B,
three kilograms, touch on a smooth

112
00:04:45,389 --> 00:04:46,289
floor.

113
00:04:46,005 --> 00:04:49,029
A twenty newton push acts on A, towards B.

114
00:04:49,029 --> 00:04:51,173
What force does B exert on A?

115
00:04:51,173 --> 00:04:52,416
Pause and solve.

116
00:04:57,816 --> 00:04:59,071
The answer is B.

117
00:04:59,071 --> 00:05:01,308
Twelve newtons, against the push.

118
00:05:01,808 --> 00:05:03,584
Both blocks move together.

119
00:05:03,584 --> 00:05:08,007
The acceleration is twenty over
five, four metres per second squared.

120
00:05:08,507 --> 00:05:11,815
The only sideways force
on B is the push from A.

121
00:05:11,815 --> 00:05:14,744
So it is three times four, twelve newtons.

122
00:05:15,244 --> 00:05:19,828
By the third law, B pushes back on A
with twelve newtons, against the push.

123
00:05:20,428 --> 00:05:21,889
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:21,889 --> 00:05:23,390
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:26,590 --> 00:05:28,520
Can you answer these without help?

126
00:05:28,920 --> 00:05:29,820
One.

127
00:05:29,448 --> 00:05:31,146
Aarav pushes his table.

128
00:05:31,146 --> 00:05:32,739
What pushes him back?

129
00:05:33,639 --> 00:05:34,539
Two.

130
00:05:34,169 --> 00:05:37,634
Why do action and reaction
never cancel each other?

131
00:05:38,534 --> 00:05:39,434
Three.

132
00:05:39,111 --> 00:05:40,822
A book rests on a table.

133
00:05:40,822 --> 00:05:43,111
What is the reaction to its weight?

134
00:05:44,011 --> 00:05:47,342
Next time: a slow truck,
or a fast cricket ball.

135
00:05:47,342 --> 00:05:49,032
Which is harder to stop?

136
00:05:49,032 --> 00:05:50,722
We find out in momentum.
